## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of Linux memory isolation, NUMA binding, and low-latency multitenant workloads under fragmentation pressure.

## Key Concepts
- memcg v2 quotas, cpuset, NUMA bindings
- HugePages/THP strategy; allocator isolation (jemalloc arenas)
- kernel memory.compaction tuning; vmscan knobs
- per-tenant BPF observability; staged rollouts

## Code Example
```c
// Minimal eBPF: count major page allocations per cgroup id
#include <uapi/linux/ptrace.h>
BPF_HASH(page_allocs, u64, u64);
int trace_alloc(struct trace_event_raw_alloc *e) {
  u64 cid = bpf_get_current_uid_gid();
  page_allocs.increment(cid);
  return 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify fragmentation impact and rollback criteria?
- Which tenants get priority during compaction?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests pragmatic SSH latency debugging in multi-tenant contexts.

## Key Concepts
- sshd_config flags: UseDNS, GSSAPIAuthentication
- DNS resolution path and NSS order
- SSH debug with -vvv and performance timing
- Safe, minimal config changes with a clear rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Node.js snippet to measure SSH login latency (sanity-check)
const {exec} = require('child_process');
const start = Date.now();
exec('ssh -o BatchMode=yes user@host exit', (e, stdout, stder) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - start;
  console.log('latency_ms', latency);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- If latency persists after UseDNS=no, what’s next?
- How would you validate rollback and minimize user impact?
